== 10. Of the Communion of Married Folks

The communion of man and wife is that duty by which they mutually and willingly share with each other both their persons and their goods, for their mutual help, need and comfort. Eph 5:28: “Husbands ought to love their wives as their own bodies. He who loves his wife loves himself.”

This duty consists chiefly in the performance of a special marital duty toward one another, and that not as a courtesy, but as a debt that is due. 1 Cor 7:3: “The husband should fulfill his marital duty to his wife, and likewise the wife to her husband.”

The marriage duty each owes the other must be shown with a singular and entire affection toward one another, and that chiefly in three ways.

First, by the right and lawful use of their bodies, that is, of the marriage bed, which is indeed an essential duty of marriage.

The marriage bed means that solitary and secret fellowship that is between man and wife alone.

And it is a thing neither commanded nor forbidden in its own nature, neither good nor bad; and so Paul counts it among things neither commanded nor forbidden, 1 Cor 7:27: “Are you committed to a wife? Do not seek to be released. Are you free of commitment? Do not look for a wife.” For this reason the Church of Rome errs in two contrary ways. First, in that it makes marriage a sacrament, and so makes every action of it good in its own nature. Secondly, in that they forbid marriage to certain persons; and the reason of the prohibition may seem to be this, that they think this secret coming together of man and wife to be filthiness.#footnote[Canon. Proposuisti, Distinct. 82.] This was the judgment of Siricius, that filthy pope of Rome. He determined that marriage was the uncleanness of the flesh, and to that end he abused the words of the apostle in Rom 8:8, affirming that those who are in the flesh, that is, in the state of matrimony, “cannot please God.” Yes, and after marriage was condemned by them, some began to detest and hate women,#footnote[Sozomen. lib. 3. cap. 16.] as Ephraim the Syrian did, of whom the church histories make mention. And the Council of Trent is of the same judgment.#footnote[Sess. 8. Canon. 9. 10.] For in setting marriage against chastity, it plainly determines that there is no chastity in marriage.

This coming together of man and wife, although it is neither commanded nor forbidden, is yet made a holy and undefiled action by the holy use of it. Heb 13:4: “Marriage is honorable among all, and the marriage bed undefiled.” And it is sanctified by the word and prayer, as all other created things and ordinances of God are. 1 Tim 4:3: “… They will forbid marriage …” and vv. 4, 5: “… nothing … should be rejected, … because it is sanctified by the word of God and prayer.” In this place we should observe how the apostle applies the point of sanctification directly to marriage.

The word of God gives direction to married folks in two ways. First, by giving them warrant that they may lawfully do this action; for whatever is not done in faith is sin, and that faith must be grounded on God’s word. Secondly, by prescribing the right and holy manner of doing it.

The holy manner stands in these particulars.

First, that it be done in moderation. For even in wedlock, excess in lusts is no better than plain adultery before God.#footnote[Ambros. lib. de Philosoph. citat. ab August. contr. Iulianum. lib. 2.] This is the judgment of the ancient church: that intemperance, that is, immoderate desire even between man and wife, is fornication.

Secondly, that it be used with a holy abstinence.

Abstinence from this secret fellowship must be kept in two cases.

First, while the woman has her monthly flow. Lev 18:19: “You must not approach a woman to uncover her nakedness during her menstrual period.” And it is made one of the marks of a good man that he does not lie with a woman in her period, Ezek 18:6.

#emph[Secondly, in the time of a solemn fast, when some grievous calamity is at hand. Then they are to give themselves (by mutual consent) to fasting and prayer,] 1 Cor 7:5: “Do not deprive each other, except by mutual consent and for a time, so you#super[+] may devote yourselves#super[+] to fasting and prayer. Then come together again, so that Satan will not tempt you#super[+] through your#super[+] lack of self-control.” 2 Sam 11:11: “Uriah answered David, ‘The ark and Israel and Judah are dwelling in tents, and my master Joab and his soldiers are camped in the open field. How can I go to my house to eat and drink and sleep with my wife? As surely as you live, and as your soul lives, I will not do such a thing!’” Zech 12:12: “The clan of the house of David will mourn apart, and their wives apart; the clan of the house of Nathan apart, and their wives apart,” and so on.

#emph[Next to the word, this action may be sanctified by prayer for a blessing on it. Children are the gift of God, and therefore married folks are not only to use the means but also to pray that they may have them,] Ps 113:9: “He settles the barren woman in her home as a joyful mother to her children.” Ps 127:3: “Children are indeed a heritage from the #smallcaps[Lord], and the fruit of the womb is His reward.” #emph[Examples of this are these:] Gen 25:21: “Isaac prayed to the #smallcaps[Lord] on behalf of his wife, because she was barren. And the #smallcaps[Lord] heard his prayer, and his wife Rebekah conceived.” 1 Sam 1:26, 27: “‘Please, my lord,’ said Hannah, ‘as surely as you live, my lord, I am the woman who stood here beside you praying to the #smallcaps[Lord].’” v. 27: “I prayed for this boy, and the #smallcaps[Lord] has granted me what I asked of Him.”

#emph[Now the fruits that are reaped and enjoyed by this holy use of the marriage bed are three.]

#set enum(numbering: "I.")

+ #emph[The having of a blessed seed.] Deut 28:1: “Now if you faithfully obey the voice of the #smallcaps[Lord] your God …,” “the fruit of your womb will be blessed, as well as the produce of your land.” #emph[This is also called] “the seed of God,” #emph[being begotten in a holy manner,] Mal 2:15: “Has not the #smallcaps[Lord] made them one, having a portion of the Spirit? And why one? Because He seeks a seed of God. So guard yourselves#super[+] in your#super[+] spirit, and do not break faith with the wife of your#super[+] youth.”
+ #emph[The preservation of the body in cleanness, so that it may be a fit temple for the Holy Spirit to dwell in,] 1 Thess 4:3, 4: “For it is God’s will that you#super[+] should be holy: … each of you#super[+] must know how to control his own body in holiness and honor, not in lustful passion like the Gentiles who do not know God.”
+ #emph[The holy state of marriage is a living type of Christ and his church, and this communion of married persons is also a figure of the union that is between him and the faithful,] Hos 2:19: “I will betroth you to Me forever; I will betroth you in righteousness and justice, in loving devotion and compassion. And I will betroth you in faithfulness, and you will know the #smallcaps[Lord].” Eph 5:23: “For the husband is the head of the wife as Christ is the head of the church, His body, of which He is the Savior.”

Here some questions are to be resolved.

#emph[Case.] I. May marriage be dissolved in the case of barrenness?

#emph[Answer.] No. For barrenness is for the most part a hidden infirmity, and one that God has often cured, even when it seemed hopeless, as in Sarah. Again, the fruit of the womb is God’s blessing and depends wholly on him. Therefore the man who rejects his wife for lack of children, when he received her at the hands of God, does wrong even to God himself.

#emph[Case] II. What if either of the married folks commits fornication, or any sin of the same kind greater than fornication, such as incest, sodomy, lying with beasts, or the like?

#emph[Answer.] Adultery and fornication are most grievous and open crimes, which break the very bond and covenant of marriage, Prov 2:17. And therefore when the married persons certainly know of them, they are by no means to be winked at, but the magistrate is to be informed of them at once. However, if the innocent party is willing to receive the adulterer again because of his repentance, then, so that the innocent party may not seem to favor and maintain sin, and to be himself one who practices uncleanness, he is to go to the congregation and declare the whole matter to the minister, so that the minister may understand the guilty party’s repentance and desire of forgiveness.

And if the adulteress has conceived and is in labor, the husband, to avoid being charged with having a bastard for his heir, is to report the adulteress’s repentance to the church, or to make certain persons acquainted with it. This is not so that she may be punished for the deed, but so that they may take notice of a child conceived in adultery, whom he may afterward lawfully disown as none of his.

When the matter is known, the innocent party may seek a divorce. For adultery is such a sin as breaks off completely not only the use but the bond and covenant of marriage. Matt 19:9: “Now I tell you#super[+] that whoever divorces his wife, except for sexual immorality, and marries another woman, commits adultery.” And yet the same bond may be continued and grow up again by the good will and consent of the innocent party; and so they may be reconciled and still live together. 1 Sam 25:44: “But Saul had given his daughter Michal, David’s wife, to Palti son of Laish, who was from Gallim.” 2 Sam 3:14: “Then David sent messengers to say to Ish-bosheth son of Saul, ‘Give me back my wife, Michal, whom I betrothed to myself for a hundred Philistine foreskins.’” The innocent may take the same course in like cases, especially on the repentance of the guilty person. But still every man must be left to his own conscience, and no one is to be compelled against his will to take back the other party, from whom he may justly be separated.

Now in seeking a divorce there is an equal right and power in both parties, so that the woman may seek it as well as the man, and he as well as she. The reason is that they are equally bound to each other and have the same right in one another’s body; provided always that the man is to maintain his superiority, and the woman to observe the modesty that befits her toward the man.

After the divorce is made, it will be lawful for the innocent party, if he does not have the gift of self-control, to marry again, on leave obtained from the church and the Christian magistrate.

But what then will become of the offending party?

#emph[Answer.] If, because his conscience is in danger for lack of the remedy, any liberty of a second marriage is to be granted him by the church or the civil magistrate (as indeed here in England there is not), several restraints must be observed in this case.

First, no such liberty is to be granted so long as it remains uncertain whether they may be reconciled, especially while the innocent party lives single. Secondly, it must plainly appear that the offending party has earnestly repented of his sin before he can either be received into the church or admitted to a second marriage. Thirdly, this liberty is not to be granted him either at once or rashly, but only after frequent and serious admonition that he should rather give himself to mourning and lamentation, and still remain alone, than desire to enjoy again the benefit of that state which he has already so shamefully abused.

So much for the first way of performing the marriage duty.

The second way is by cherishing one another. Eph 5:29: “No one ever hated his own flesh, but he nourishes and cherishes it, just as Christ does the church.” This cherishing is the performing of any duties that tend to the preserving of each other’s life. For this reason they are freely to share with each other their goods, their counsel and their labors, for the good of themselves and their own.

#emph[The third way is by a holy kind of rejoicing and solacing themselves with each other, in a mutual showing of the signs and tokens of love and kindness.] Prov 5:18: “May you rejoice in the wife of your youth.” v. 19: “A loving doe, a graceful fawn—may her breasts satisfy you always; may you be captivated by her love forever.” Song 1:2: “Let him kiss me with the kisses of his mouth! For your love is more delightful than wine.” Gen 26:8: “When Isaac had been there a long time, Abimelech king of the Philistines looked down from the window and was surprised to see Isaac caressing his wife Rebekah. Abimelech sent for Isaac and said, ‘So she is really your wife!’” Isa 62:5: “For as a young man marries a young woman, so your sons will marry you; and as a bridegroom rejoices over his bride, so your God will rejoice over you.”

This rejoicing and delight is permitted more to the man than to the woman; and to them both, more in their young years than in their old age.
